INSPECTION WORKS; LOADING DOES NOT, YET.  halt, read, resume against a
running kernel is solid and is what this was wanted for.

load_inferno -- halt, load kernel8.img at 0x80000, set the pc -- does
not work, and the config says why at length.  A kernel entry expects
the machine as the firmware leaves it: MMU off, caches off, nothing
mapped.  Halting a live Inferno leaves the MMU on and its page tables
live, so the jump faults on the first instruction (esr fffb7fb9, pc
00080000).  Making it work means clearing SCTLR_EL1's M/C/I bits
first, which OpenOCD's aarch64 target does not expose through "reg".

And a Pi cannot be reset over JTAG into a waiting state: reset_config
is trst_only, TRST resets the TAP and not the SoC, and anything that
does reset the SoC hands control back to the VideoCore firmware, which
loads whatever is on the card.  So installing a kernel still means the
card, for now.

WHAT IT WILL NOT DO.  It does not reset the machine, on purpose: a
reset hands control back to the VideoCore firmware, which loads
whatever is on the card and undoes the point.  And it does not replace
the serial console -- the kernel's own prints still go out the PL011,
and that is still the fastest way to see what it thinks it is doing.

Only core 0 is examined at startup.  The kernel parks cores 1-3 at WFE
in l.s before anything else runs, because the SoC starts all four at
0x80000; a parked core has a debug interface but nothing to say.
